# Development mode: debug builds only (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-15), verbatim:

> **Hard Rule: We are in development mode. There is no reason to build a
> release build even for use in the MSI.**

This is a **speed** decision, not a correctness one. Nothing about the
product's behavior requires the release profile while the workspace is in
development mode, and the release profile costs roughly an order of
magnitude more wall clock per iteration on the largest crate in the tree.

## The rule

> **While the workspace is in development mode, every build an agent runs
> uses the Cargo dev/debug profile -- local dev deploys and MSI packaging
> alike. Agents must not pass `--release`, `-Configuration Release`, or
> `-ReleaseBuild`, and must not invoke a script path whose default profile
> is release, unless the operator explicitly asks for a release build in
> that same message. Scripts keep an explicit opt-in switch for the
> operator; the default is debug.**

## Why -- the measured cost

`pim-offline-server/Cargo.toml` declares, for `[profile.release]`:

| Setting | Value | Consequence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `incremental` | `false` | Every touch of the crate is a from-scratch compile |
| `codegen-units` | `16` | Little parallelism across codegen units |
| `opt-level` | `3` | Full optimization on a very large library crate |

Because `build.rs` copies `ui/dist` into `ui-dist` and `src/static_files.rs`
embeds it with `include_dir!`, **any admin-SPA bundle change invalidates the
Rust crate** and forces that from-scratch opt-level-3 recompile. Measured on
the lab host: **roughly 10+ minutes per iteration**.

The dev profile has `incremental = true` and `codegen-units = 256`, and
`target\debug` is already warm. The same change set rebuilds in a small
fraction of that time.

## Scope

| Surface | Default profile while in development mode |
| --- | --- |
| `cargo build` / `cargo run` run by an agent | **debug** (no `--release`) |
| `pim-offline-server/scripts/Deploy-OfflineServerDev.ps1` | **debug** (`-ReleaseBuild` opts in) |
| `pim-offline-client/scripts/Deploy-OfflineAgentDev.ps1` | **debug** (`-ReleaseBuild` opts in) |
| `pim-installers/scripts/Build-Pim*Msi.ps1` | **debug preferred** (`-Configuration Release` opts in); falls back to a release artifact when no debug artifact exists in a sibling repo |
| Operator-requested release build, named in the same message | release |
| Customer / partner / public share | release -- see the public-share carve-out below |

Artifact paths follow the profile: `target\debug\...` rather than
`target\release\...`. That includes the staged AWS-LC FIPS DLL
(see [`fips-dll-staging.mdc`](fips-dll-staging.mdc)) -- the
`aws_lc_fips_*_crypto.dll` must sit next to the **debug** binaries too, or
the service fails to start on Windows with a missing-DLL error.

## Forbidden constructs

- `cargo build --release` / `cargo run --release` / `cargo test --release`
  in any agent-run command or default script path.
- `-Configuration Release` or `-ReleaseBuild` passed by an agent on its own
  initiative.
- Adding a new build or deploy script whose default profile is release.
- **Editing a `[profile.dev]` or `[profile.release]` table to "make release
  faster."** Profile settings feed Cargo's metadata hash: changing one
  invalidates the entire dependency graph in both directions and forces a
  full rebuild -- including `aws-lc-fips-sys`, which
  [`never-rebuild-aws-lc.mdc`](never-rebuild-aws-lc.mdc) forbids rebuilding
  at all. **The fix is to stop using release, not to retune it.**
- Reaching for `cargo clean` or deleting `target/**` to "reset" after a
  profile mix-up. Both profiles coexist; keep them both warm.
- Using this rule as a reason to skip a UI build. It changes the **Rust
  profile only** -- see the cross-reference below.
- Calling a debug-profile MSI a signed release artifact.

## Required agent behavior

1. Default every build to the dev profile. Omit `--release`; let scripts use
   their debug default.
2. When a script needs a profile-specific path, resolve it from the profile
   in use rather than hard-coding `target\release`.
3. Verify the FIPS DLL is staged next to the debug binary before starting a
   service, per [`fips-dll-staging.mdc`](fips-dll-staging.mdc).
4. If the operator asks for a release build, say plainly that this rule is
   being set aside for that request, and do it.
5. When a sibling repo has no debug artifact and packaging would break,
   fall back to that repo's release artifact and say so -- do not fail the
   packaging run, and do not silently claim a full debug build.
6. Mirror this rule to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same change set
   ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)) -- the workspace
   `.cursor` directory is not a git root.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation.** An agent changes one admin-SPA page, rebuilds the Vite
bundle, and then runs:

```powershell
cargo build --release --features skip-ui-build --bin pim-offline-server
powershell .\scripts\Deploy-OfflineServerDev.ps1 -SkipUiBuild
```

The embedded `ui-dist` changed, so the release profile recompiles the whole
`pim-offline-server` library at `opt-level = 3` with `incremental = false`.
Ten-plus minutes pass before the operator can look at a one-line UI change.
The agent then "optimizes" by setting `[profile.release] opt-level = 1`,
which flips the Cargo metadata hash and triggers a second full-graph rebuild
including the locked AWS-LC FIPS module.

**Fix.** Build the same change set on the dev profile and deploy with the
debug default:

```powershell
cargo build --features skip-ui-build --bin pim-offline-server
powershell .\scripts\Deploy-OfflineServerDev.ps1 -SkipUiBuild
```

`target\debug` is incremental and already warm, so the iteration is a small
fraction of the release cost. The `[profile.*]` tables are left untouched,
so no fingerprint flips and AWS-LC is never rebuilt.

